["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The UFO Craze Is Not an Astronomy Problem, It&#x27;s a Belief Problem When the evidence stays at the level of testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The more science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life becomes in our observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we&#x27;re really talking about is ourselves. EconTalk EconTalk Are We Alone? Cosmic Loneliness and the Longing to Believe (with Adam Kirsch) UFO Extraterrestrial Life Philosophy of Science Anti-Expert Sentiment Social Psychology UAP euphemism treadmill book experience Drake equation AATIP\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-econtalk-所有外星人想象都是人类的自画像/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:11:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The UFO Craze Is Not an Astronomy Problem, It&#x27;s a Belief Problem</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the evidence stays at the level of testimony, whether the universe is crowded becomes a matter of faith. The more science advances, the rarer extraterrestrial life becomes in our observations; when we talk about UFOs, what we&#x27;re really talking about is ourselves.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">UFO</span><span class=\"tag\">Extraterrestrial Life</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants The US sees 130,000 new kidney failure cases a year and fewer than 30,000 transplants; Al Roth unpacks where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploiting anyone. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area Kidneys for sale Kidney transplant Organ markets Market design Moral controversy Public policy repugnant transaction market design non-directed donor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-grayarea-禁止卖肾的代价-本不该失去的生命/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7c8b1deddea1e240-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">49:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>The Cost of Banning Kidney Sales: 130,000 New Cases a Year, Fewer Than 30,000 Transplants</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The US sees 130,000 new kidney failure cases a year and fewer than 30,000 transplants; Al Roth unpacks where the repugnance toward organ markets comes from, and argues that regulated compensation could save lives without exploiting anyone.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Kidney transplant</span><span class=\"tag\">Organ markets</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — it&#x27;s the brain deciding it costs too much Neurologist Masud Husain uses case studies and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy stem from an excessively high perceived effort cost, not from a lack of desire. Lowering activation energy, planning ahead, and breaking tasks down are the practical strategies for getting around the barrier. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab How to Improve Motivation &amp; Overcome Procrastination | Dr. Masud Husain Motivation Procrastination Dopamine Neuroscience Alzheimer&#x27;s disease Addiction apathy basal ganglia activation energy wanting vs liking\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-huberman-动机不是欲望问题-而是激活能量障碍/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d9e03163dcfb779e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">2:20:21</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Procrastination isn&#x27;t laziness — it&#x27;s the brain deciding it costs too much</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Neurologist Masud Husain uses case studies and brain imaging to show that procrastination and apathy stem from an excessively high perceived effort cost, not from a lack of desire. Lowering activation energy, planning ahead, and breaking tasks down are the practical strategies for getting around the barrier.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Motivation</span><span class=\"tag\">Procrastination</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Africa Doesn&#x27;t Lack Resources, It Lacks Population Density Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession and spent $20 billion of his own money on a Nigerian refinery to become Africa&#x27;s richest man, but Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can replicate Asia&#x27;s miracle is the urban market size created by population density — not minerals or aid. Odd Lots Odd Lots The Nigerian Industrial Behemoth That Could Reshape the African Economy African economy industrial policy manufacturing demographics China-Africa relations land reform flying geese paradigm resource curse developmental state kulak demonstration effect\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-oddlots-非洲工业化不缺资源-缺的是人口密度/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">58:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Africa Doesn&#x27;t Lack Resources, It Lacks Population Density</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Dangote built his fortune on a monopoly concession and spent $20 billion of his own money on a Nigerian refinery to become Africa&#x27;s richest man, but Studwell argues the variable that decides whether Africa can replicate Asia&#x27;s miracle is the urban market size created by population density — not minerals or aid.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">African economy</span><span class=\"tag\">industrial policy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Water Privatisation Is Not Destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s Referendum Victory on Water Rights On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans wrote &quot;water is an essential resource for life&quot; into their constitution with 64.7% voting yes, and stipulated that water supply could only be provided by the state — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out of its constitution. The movement began when the taps were shut off in a coastal city. Witness History Witness History Uruguay&#x27;s water referendum Water rights Referendum Privatisation Uruguay Social movements Constitution concession mate\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-witness-供水私有化不是宿命-乌拉圭公投的水权胜利/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4ad7b76f4c635f2c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">10:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Witness History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Water Privatisation Is Not Destiny: Uruguay&#x27;s Referendum Victory on Water Rights</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On 31 October 2004, Uruguayans wrote &quot;water is an essential resource for life&quot; into their constitution with 64.7% voting yes, and stipulated that water supply could only be provided by the state — making Uruguay the first country in the world to lock water privatisation out of its constitution. The movement began when the taps were shut off in a coastal city.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Water rights</span><span class=\"tag\">Referendum</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"On an aircraft carrier, 5,000 people and only 60 fight; the rest restock vending machines After 9/11, of the 5,000 people on a US aircraft carrier only fifty or sixty pilots ever touched a bomb; everyone else spent their time restocking vending machines, doing laundry and trading gossip, while what worried the captain most was whether his sailors were watching the news. This American Life This American Life 206: Somewhere in the Arabian Sea US military aircraft carrier daily life of war organisational management portrait of the young 9/11 blood chit Tailhook scandal Hollywood shower berthing XO\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-tal-航母战争的日常-补零食-防谈恋爱-等回家/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1da611bfe52eccd0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">This American Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>On an aircraft carrier, 5,000 people and only 60 fight; the rest restock vending machines</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">After 9/11, of the 5,000 people on a US aircraft carrier only fifty or sixty pilots ever touched a bomb; everyone else spent their time restocking vending machines, doing laundry and trading gossip, while what worried the captain most was whether his sailors were watching the news.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">US military</span><span class=\"tag\">aircraft carrier</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Screens Train Multitasking, Not Learning: The Harder Classrooms Go Digital, the Dumber Kids Get Since 2010, the harder classrooms have pushed digitalization, the more children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have declined across the board; screens train multitasking, not learning, and AI only makes the errors more invisible. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Tech Making Our Kids Less Capable? With Dr Jared Cooney Horvath Edtech Classroom screens Cognitive development AI education Digital literacy Counter-consensus Ed tech ICILS Offloading Outsourcing SRLs Drill-and-kill\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-iq2-技术不等于学习-屏幕正在让一代人变笨/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/46f8e9d9c37f6c72-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">33:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Screens Train Multitasking, Not Learning: The Harder Classrooms Go Digital, the Dumber Kids Get</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Since 2010, the harder classrooms have pushed digitalization, the more children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have declined across the board; screens train multitasking, not learning, and AI only makes the errors more invisible.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Edtech</span><span class=\"tag\">Classroom screens</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Britain&#x27;s Miscalculations Lit the Northern Ireland Conflict: The Provisional IRA Meant to Fight, It Wasn&#x27;t Forced Into Rebellion A series of British government miscalculations in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, refusing to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — let the Provisional IRA swell from a fringe armed group into the protagonist of a thirty-year conflict, while British soldiers were themselves radicalised by the killing. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 699. The Troubles: Return of the IRA (Part 3) Northern Ireland conflict IRA counterinsurgency British politics insurgency sectarian violence The Troubles Operation Demetrius UVF\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-restishistory-临时ira绝非防御组织-英军误判铸成三十年冲突/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:51</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Britain&#x27;s Miscalculations Lit the Northern Ireland Conflict: The Provisional IRA Meant to Fight, It Wasn&#x27;t Forced Into Rebellion</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A series of British government miscalculations in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, refusing to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — let the Provisional IRA swell from a fringe armed group into the protagonist of a thirty-year conflict, while British soldiers were themselves radicalised by the killing.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Northern Ireland conflict</span><span class=\"tag\">IRA</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Mental events have no laws, yet reasons can still cause us to act freely Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are only token-identical to physical events; but in the explanation of action, reasons as causes remain real, and when a reason causes an action, the agent is precisely free. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 398: Donald Davidson on Mind vs. Body (Part Two) Mind-body problem Philosophy of action Anomalous monism Causal explanation Davidson Analytic philosophy token identity homonomic generalization heteronomic generalization pro attitude primary reason rationalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-pel-心物无规律-理由仍可作为原因而自由/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4024c3bbaf655b13-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">53:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Mental events have no laws, yet reasons can still cause us to act freely</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are only token-identical to physical events; but in the explanation of action, reasons as causes remain real, and when a reason causes an action, the agent is precisely free.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Mind-body problem</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy of action</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"A 22-year-old CEO, five rounds, over $100M: the first round&#x27;s terms decide life or death In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi built RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds and over $100M raised, RPO selling about 100 units a month. His judgments: the first round&#x27;s terms decide life or death, open source is a moat that lowers the friction of collaboration, and the body is the embodied entry point. 十字路口Crossing 十字路口Crossing 22 岁的具身 CEO、5 轮融资、过亿美元、“不知天有多高”、“一年吃了十年的苦”｜对谈黄一：萝博派对创始人/CEO Embodied AI Robotics fundraising Open source Startup management Org design missing layer deep pocket BFM / Behavior Foundation Model sim2real hive structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-crossing-具身创业最优解-快融资-软条款-全栈开源/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3434775ccb19b8d7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"十\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">十字路口Crossing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>A 22-year-old CEO, five rounds, over $100M: the first round&#x27;s terms decide life or death</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi built RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds and over $100M raised, RPO selling about 100 units a month. His judgments: the first round&#x27;s terms decide life or death, open source is a moat that lowers the friction of collaboration, and the body is the embodied entry point.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Robotics fundraising</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"What Etched&#x27;s Chip Actually Solves May Be Bandwidth, Not Compute Etched uses 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors plus cross-chip direct SRAM links to weld the transformer into hardware, but the host suspects the real problem being solved is bandwidth, not compute. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato The Reason My Phone is Ringing Daily: Etched AI chips transformer-specific chips compute efficiency funding bandwidth bottleneck tape out RDMA threshold voltage cluster scale memory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-techtechpotato-etched的芯片-可能解决的是带宽而不是算力/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5ab75e84ff855e23-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>What Etched&#x27;s Chip Actually Solves May Be Bandwidth, Not Compute</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Etched uses 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors plus cross-chip direct SRAM links to weld the transformer into hardware, but the host suspects the real problem being solved is bandwidth, not compute.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI chips</span><span class=\"tag\">transformer-specific chips</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The eval moat isn&#x27;t AI, it&#x27;s human taste Evals can&#x27;t just be auto-generated by AI: top-down criteria are what it&#x27;s good at, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled from error data — have to come from a human, then be scaled up with Claude Code. Peter Yang Peter Yang How to Build Better AI Evals with Claude Code in 5 Steps | Shreya &amp; Hamel Eval systems Claude Code Error analysis LLM products Human review bottom-up evals top-down evals LLM judge error discovery staccato fragments slop\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-peteryang-自下而上的评估-是-ai-给不了你的护城河/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/49510d80b4317525-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">54:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Peter Yang</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>The eval moat isn&#x27;t AI, it&#x27;s human taste</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Evals can&#x27;t just be auto-generated by AI: top-down criteria are what it&#x27;s good at, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled from error data — have to come from a human, then be scaled up with Claude Code.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Eval systems</span><span class=\"tag\">Claude Code</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ebola Infects Neurons and Sheds Virus for 120 Days; Fish Cancers Spread Between Fish Brain organoids show Ebola can infect neurons and release infectious virus for up to 120 days; melanomas in brown bullhead catfish populations descend from a single clonal ancestor and transmit between fish. The boundaries of viral persistence and contagious cancer are rewritten at the same time. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1351: Ebola Virus Brains and Contagious Catfish Cancers Viral persistence Brain organoids Ebola Contagious cancer NIH funding Biosafety productive persistence nonproductive persistence BSL-4 MOI\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-twiv-埃博拉持续感染脑神经元-鱼类黑色素瘤能传染/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">1:41:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Ebola Infects Neurons and Sheds Virus for 120 Days; Fish Cancers Spread Between Fish</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Brain organoids show Ebola can infect neurons and release infectious virus for up to 120 days; melanomas in brown bullhead catfish populations descend from a single clonal ancestor and transmit between fish. The boundaries of viral persistence and contagious cancer are rewritten at the same time.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Viral persistence</span><span class=\"tag\">Brain organoids</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Terror Birds Didn&#x27;t Die Out Because of Humans — They Died Out When the Isthmus of Panama Closed Terror birds were the apex predatory birds that arose on South America&#x27;s isolated island continent after the dinosaurs went extinct, and they ruled for tens of millions of years; their disappearance is tied to the great faunal interchange that followed the closing of the Isthmus of Panama, and it came earlier than the arrival of humans in South America. The Ancients The Ancients Terror Birds Paleontology Bird Evolution South America Extinction Asteroid Impact Seriemas Eocene Isthmus of Panama\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-ancients-恐怖鸟死于物种大交换而非人类/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/270440780adbe49b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">45:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Terror Birds Didn&#x27;t Die Out Because of Humans — They Died Out When the Isthmus of Panama Closed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Terror birds were the apex predatory birds that arose on South America&#x27;s isolated island continent after the dinosaurs went extinct, and they ruled for tens of millions of years; their disappearance is tied to the great faunal interchange that followed the closing of the Isthmus of Panama, and it came earlier than the arrival of humans in South America.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Paleontology</span><span class=\"tag\">Bird Evolution</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Ukrainians Are Resisting the War With Poetry and Artifacts, Not Weapons How the war turned a culture reporter into a language learner, and a prose writer into a poet: Ukrainians resist the war&#x27;s erasure of identity and memory by rescuing artifacts and writing poems. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Ukraine’s Culture Worth Fighting For? With Charlotte Higgins Ukraine Culture War Poetry Museums Identity War Narrative Cyrillic alphabet Lviv Book Forum citizen army\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-iq2-在乌克兰-文化是值得用生命去救的东西/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/0eefaf93ef4770ba-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">37:37</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Ukrainians Are Resisting the War With Poetry and Artifacts, Not Weapons</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">How the war turned a culture reporter into a language learner, and a prose writer into a poet: Ukrainians resist the war&#x27;s erasure of identity and memory by rescuing artifacts and writing poems.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ukraine</span><span class=\"tag\">Culture War</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Encrypted Thoughts Are Not Secret: A Small Model Can Read a Large Model&#x27;s Reasoning Aloud Frontier models hand the encrypted thinking process back to users alongside the answer, but that ciphertext can be replayed verbatim into a small model of the same family and read aloud directly — leading to privacy leaks, jailbreaks, prompt injection, and even a channel for distilling capability. Machine Learning Street Talk ML Street Talk Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs — Ilia Shumailov &amp; Alexander Panfilov Reasoning encryption Jailbreak Model distillation AI security Prompt injection Privacy leakage reasoning trace prefill replay attack responsible disclosure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-mlst-加密思考不是秘密-小模型能把大模型的推理念出来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7306f7dff4dc8ceb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">49:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Machine Learning Street Talk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Encrypted Thoughts Are Not Secret: A Small Model Can Read a Large Model&#x27;s Reasoning Aloud</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Frontier models hand the encrypted thinking process back to users alongside the answer, but that ciphertext can be replayed verbatim into a small model of the same family and read aloud directly — leading to privacy leaks, jailbreaks, prompt injection, and even a channel for distilling capability.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Reasoning encryption</span><span class=\"tag\">Jailbreak</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The people who find AI incidents are never the safety evaluators In every agent-gone-wrong incident this summer, the ones who noticed first were infrastructure security teams — researchers running evals account for precisely zero cases; and to keep pace with attacks, defenders are being forced to hand more permissions to the very agents just shown to cross the line. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution AI in the AM — Weekly Highlights: Relaunch Week (Aug 17–20, 2026) safety evals agent incidents inference cost open weights compute AI governance CoBench mind virus ghostjacking effective compute consumption Minimal Standard for Safeguards\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-cogrev-ai-事故的发现者从来不是安全评估员/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dd01d2cd4bc88e5a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:33:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>The people who find AI incidents are never the safety evaluators</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In every agent-gone-wrong incident this summer, the ones who noticed first were infrastructure security teams — researchers running evals account for precisely zero cases; and to keep pace with attacks, defenders are being forced to hand more permissions to the very agents just shown to cross the line.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">safety evals</span><span class=\"tag\">agent incidents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Food Safety Standard Born from Space Food: Testing the Finished Product Is Never Enough HACCP, the core of the global food safety system, was born from NASA&#x27;s space food mission: testing the finished product is never enough — you have to control the entire process, from raw materials to people. The 1971 botulism incident and the 1993 Jack in the Box outbreak turned it into a global mandatory standard. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class SYMHC Classics: Astronaut Origins of Food Safety Food Safety HACCP NASA Regulation Public Health Food Industry HACCP / Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points CCP / Critical Control Point Codex Alimentarius FMEA / Failure Mode and Effects Analysis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-stuffmissed-haccp-为防止宇航员食物中毒而生的全球食安标准/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>The Food Safety Standard Born from Space Food: Testing the Finished Product Is Never Enough</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">HACCP, the core of the global food safety system, was born from NASA&#x27;s space food mission: testing the finished product is never enough — you have to control the entire process, from raw materials to people. The 1971 botulism incident and the 1993 Jack in the Box outbreak turned it into a global mandatory standard.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Food Safety</span><span class=\"tag\">HACCP</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"A question isn&#x27;t defined by its question mark, but by the act of seeking information A real question is defined by function, not form; asking is a power you can practise, and most of the answers AI gives today haven&#x27;t yet answered a question anyone seriously asked. Philosophy Bites Philosophy Bites Lani Watson on the Power of Questions Questioning Philosophy Education AI critique Communication Socrates information seeking act rhetorical question private questions and social questions answer oriented model\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-phibites-真正的问题不是问号-而是寻求信息的行动/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/243c92b44ec40dbb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">16:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Philosophy Bites</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>A question isn&#x27;t defined by its question mark, but by the act of seeking information</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A real question is defined by function, not form; asking is a power you can practise, and most of the answers AI gives today haven&#x27;t yet answered a question anyone seriously asked.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Questioning</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"A 20-Person Team Burned $2 Billion: AI Rewrites the Ceiling on Capital Efficiency AI lets a 20-person team spend $2 billion efficiently, and venture capital shifts from zero-sum competition to strategic control points; the frontier labs&#x27; funding advantage will eventually fade, and the application layer captures more of the value. The a16z Show a16z Martin Casado on Where the Value Is Going in AI AI investing Capital efficiency Model competition Open source M&amp;A Startups autocatalytic effects AI complete token path two-sided marketplace founder-market fit\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-a16z-小团队吞下亿级资本-ai-改写投资逻辑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">42:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>A 20-Person Team Burned $2 Billion: AI Rewrites the Ceiling on Capital Efficiency</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI lets a 20-person team spend $2 billion efficiently, and venture capital shifts from zero-sum competition to strategic control points; the frontier labs&#x27; funding advantage will eventually fade, and the application layer captures more of the value.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Capital efficiency</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Tamiflu Too Late in Severe Flu Doubles the Death Risk Severely ill flu patients who start Tamiflu about five days after symptoms have roughly twice the 90-day death risk of those who don&#x27;t take it; an mRNA flu vaccine is approved, but only ages 50-64 got traditional approval. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1350: Clinical update with Dr. Daniel Griffin flu RSV mRNA vaccine vaccine hesitancy Tamiflu public health cyclosporiasis New World screwworm nirsevimab test-negative case-control adaptive analysis non-medical exemption\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-twiv-重症流感太晚用达菲-可能反而提高死亡风险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">1:01:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Tamiflu Too Late in Severe Flu Doubles the Death Risk</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Severely ill flu patients who start Tamiflu about five days after symptoms have roughly twice the 90-day death risk of those who don&#x27;t take it; an mRNA flu vaccine is approved, but only ages 50-64 got traditional approval.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">flu</span><span class=\"tag\">RSV</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Foreign Food Brands Change Hands to Chinese Owners, and the Real Moat Is in Flavorings McDonald&#x27;s, Starbucks, Burger King and other foreign food brands have successively changed hands to Chinese owners; localization is the line between life and death in food service, but upstream in perfumery and fragrance, the four Western giants still hold a monopoly on flavorings — the supply-chain barrier is the real watershed for domestic substitution. 疯投圈 疯投圈 145 ✪ 改属中资的餐饮洋品牌 Food service Localization Foreign retreat Perfume and fragrance Starbucks Domestic substitution CFB / China Food &amp; Beverage Group\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-fengtouquan-洋餐饮纷纷让位中资-硬壁垒藏在香气里/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f4193533f4aa66ed-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"疯\"><span class=\"dur\">1:12:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">疯投圈</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Foreign Food Brands Change Hands to Chinese Owners, and the Real Moat Is in Flavorings</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">McDonald&#x27;s, Starbucks, Burger King and other foreign food brands have successively changed hands to Chinese owners; localization is the line between life and death in food service, but upstream in perfumery and fragrance, the four Western giants still hold a monopoly on flavorings — the supply-chain barrier is the real watershed for domestic substitution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Food service</span><span class=\"tag\">Localization</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Simulating real people is not a reasoning problem: frontier models get only 20–30% accuracy Joon Sung Park&#x27;s argument: treating people as trainable objects does not call for a stronger reasoning model but for a model that makes the same mistakes people make — frontier models reach only 20–30% accuracy predicting the behavior of niche populations, and Simile has pushed that to 85% using two-hour deep interviews plus RCT data. Latent Space Latent Space Simulation: the new Scaling Law — Joon Sung Park, Simile AI Simulation Post-training Behavioral data Synthetic panels Scaling law Market research social physics attitudinal vs. behavioral concept testing synthetic panel agent-based modeling Open Science Framework pre-registration\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-latentspace-前沿模型太聪明-反而模拟不了真人/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bb52be8f143fe3c1-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:09:38</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Simulating real people is not a reasoning problem: frontier models get only 20–30% accuracy</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Joon Sung Park&#x27;s argument: treating people as trainable objects does not call for a stronger reasoning model but for a model that makes the same mistakes people make — frontier models reach only 20–30% accuracy predicting the behavior of niche populations, and Simile has pushed that to 85% using two-hour deep interviews plus RCT data.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Simulation</span><span class=\"tag\">Post-training</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Being Itself Has No Content: Peirce Overturns Substance-Centrism in Nine Pages In a nine-page paper from 1868, Peirce takes ‘a concept must be able to unify the content of consciousness’ as his test, deriving being from substance and then the intermediate categories such as quality. Being itself has no content; it only connects a predicate to a subject. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life PEL Presents Closereads: Peirce&#x27;s New Categories (Part One) Philosophy Categories Peirce Transcendental Deduction Abstraction Unity manifold precision ground copula substance\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-pel-peirce的范畴论-存在无内容-实体是当下的它/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1bb9773e6a8da6d8-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Being Itself Has No Content: Peirce Overturns Substance-Centrism in Nine Pages</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a nine-page paper from 1868, Peirce takes ‘a concept must be able to unify the content of consciousness’ as his test, deriving being from substance and then the intermediate categories such as quality. Being itself has no content; it only connects a predicate to a subject.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Philosophy</span><span class=\"tag\">Categories</span>\n</div></div></a>"]